What:		/sys/kernel/security/medusa/status
Date:		July 2026
Contact:	Jano Kacer <jano.kacer@gmail.com>
Description:
		Root-readable, read-only snapshot of Medusa decision-engine
		liveness. One key=value record is emitted per line:

		kernel_release
			The running kernel release.
		protocol_version
			The active Medusa userspace protocol version.
		authorization_server
			"handshaking" while a userspace transport is processing
			the announced schema, "connected" after the READY exchange
			has completed, or "disconnected".
		authorization_server_name
			The active or handshaking kernel transport name, or "none".
		protocol_state
			"disconnected", "handshaking", or "ready".
		policy_readiness
			"unavailable", "initializing", or "ready". Protocol v3
			READY is emitted by Constable only after its policy _init()
			handler completes.
		authorization_server_health
			"healthy", "unhealthy", "unknown", or "unavailable".
		circuit_breaker
			"closed", "open", "unknown", "not_ready", or
			"not_connected".
		health_reason
			"healthy", "disconnected", "decision_timeout",
			"overloaded", "initializing", "unknown", or "invalid".
		policy_generation
			The current kernel context-invalidation generation. It
			advances when a ready server registers or disconnects.
		active_policy_generation
			The generation eligible to answer decisions, or zero while
			disconnected or handshaking.
		last_ready_policy_generation
			The most recent generation that completed READY. It remains
			available across a disconnect and incomplete handshake.
		pending_requests
			Number of requests currently awaiting userspace.
		pending_limit
			Maximum number of simultaneous pending requests.
		decision_lease_ms
			Initial and renewal liveness lease for each request.
		protocol_replies
			Successfully matched decision replies.
		protocol_lease_renewals
			Successfully matched decision-progress messages.
		protocol_malformed_messages
			Frames rejected for an invalid length.
		protocol_invalid_answers
			Decision replies rejected for an unsupported verdict.
		protocol_unknown_commands
			Frames rejected for an unknown command.
		protocol_unknown_requests
			Replies or renewals naming no pending request.
		protocol_stale_requests
			Replies or renewals rejected for a stale generation.

		Protocol counters are cumulative from boot and use wrapping
		unsigned 64-bit arithmetic. Each rejected protocol frame also
		emits a rate-limited ``Medusa: op=protocol_error`` audit record.
		The record identifies the protocol and policy generation, stable
		error-kind name, command and request ID when available, negative
		errno, per-kind sequence, and number suppressed since the
		previous emitted record. Error kinds are limited independently
		to three records per five seconds, so one class of malformed input
		cannot hide the first occurrence of another class.

		The file mode is 0400. It does not expose policy mutation.
		Medusa's own delegated file-open policy is bypassed so the file
		remains available during an authorization-server outage; normal
		VFS permissions and other active LSMs still apply.

What:		/sys/kernel/security/medusa/events
Date:		July 2026
Contact:	Jano Kacer <jano.kacer@gmail.com>
Description:
		Root-readable, read-only snapshot of registered Medusa events.
		Each line contains the event and subject/object class names,
		runtime trigger bit and bitmap owner, installed fallback policy,
		and cumulative decision counters:

		decisions
			Calls entering the central decision engine.
		delegated
			Calls for which a userspace request was issued.
		baseline
			Final verdicts selected by the installed baseline.
		online_required
			Final denials selected because the event requires userspace.
		allowed, denied
			Final central-engine verdicts.
		timed_out
			Final fallbacks caused by an expired decision lease.
		invalid_replies
			Final denials caused by an unsupported server verdict.
		degraded_decisions
			Final fallbacks caused by any unavailability reason.

		Event and class names are the protocol-v3 names announced to
		Constable. "event_bit" is a runtime trigger position, not a stable
		numeric event identifier. Counters use wrapping unsigned 64-bit
		arithmetic. Kernel-cached fast-path accesses do not enter the central
		decision engine and are therefore not included; hook-level cached
		accounting is a separate future ABI.

		The file mode is 0400. It does not expose policy mutation.
		Medusa's own delegated file-open policy is bypassed so the file
		remains available during an authorization-server outage; normal
		VFS permissions and other active LSMs still apply.
